THE TWO FINDINGS, AND WHAT WAS DONE (opus-5, r210)

N-1  cor:pinch claimed more than thm:const proves.
     "Eventually constant" admits finitely many exceptional weights; the theorem admits
     only w_0.  The corollary was labelled "immediate", and it is not.
     => REPAIRED, and the broad statement KEPT, because it is the statement
        Conjecture 1's first branch has to cite.  Two-line argument added as a proof:
        the exceptional weights contribute a perturbation bounded uniformly in k on
        0 <= theta <= 3pi/2k, while the main term grows like k; (c) survives with A
        replaced by A+B, and the lower end survives because the main term only falls
        below B within O(k^-2) of the rung.  Rate unchanged: t_1 = pi/2k (1+O(1/k)).
        The STATUS now says explicitly that it is NOT immediate.
     => And the accompanying remark records where the same estimate fails: the moment
        infinitely many weights differ from w, which is exactly where sec 4 begins.

N-2  conj:line's two branches do not exhaust R = 1.
     Profiles decaying more slowly than every power (w_j = 1/log(j+2)) fall in neither,
     and the display read as a dichotomy.
     => REPAIRED by grammar, per F36: one sentence marking the classes as illustrative
        and not exhaustive, stating where we expect the intermediate classes to fall,
        and saying that this expectation is NOT part of the conjecture because it is
        measured at exactly one profile.

